Chapter: CceRoundtrip
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 CCE is the compiler's internal encoding and the boundary every piece
 of text crosses. to-unicode and from-unicode must be inverses wherever
 the mapping exists, so this counts mismatches over ranges rather than
 spot checking a few characters.

 It also counts how many points are MAPPED. Without that the test is
 vacuous: a from-unicode answering minus one for everything would show
 zero round-trip failures and look perfect, which is the same shape as
 a function that always answers the same thing.

 The four named characters are the ones CLAUDE.md documents, kept here
 because a rule in that file rests on them: the em-dash does have a CCE
 code point and does round-trip, while a carriage return genuinely does
 not map. If those ever change, the argument in the rule changes with
 them.

Section: Ranges

  rt-fail : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rt-fail (u) (n) (acc) =
    if u >= n then acc
    else let c = from-unicode u
    in let bad = if c < 0 then 0 else (if to-unicode c == u then 0 else 1)
    in rt-fail (u + 1) n (acc + bad)

  mapped : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mapped (u) (n) (acc) =
    if u >= n then acc
    else mapped (u + 1) n (acc + (if from-unicode u < 0 then 0 else 1))

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("ascii-mapped=" & show (mapped 0 128 0))
    print-line-uni ("ascii-rt-fail=" & show (rt-fail 0 128 0))
    print-line-uni ("low1k-mapped=" & show (mapped 0 1024 0))
    print-line-uni ("low1k-rt-fail=" & show (rt-fail 0 1024 0))
    print-line-uni ("punct-mapped=" & show (mapped 8192 8704 0))
    print-line-uni ("punct-rt-fail=" & show (rt-fail 8192 8704 0))
    print-line-uni ("emdash=" & show (from-unicode 8212) & " back=" & show (to-unicode (from-unicode 8212)))
    print-line-uni ("carriage-return=" & show (from-unicode 13))
    print-line-uni ("space=" & show (from-unicode 32) & " upper-a=" & show (from-unicode 65) & " digit-zero=" & show (from-unicode 48))
  end

